            memcpy (names[ct_name], buf + 3 + tmp_size + 1, cb);
        }
        ct_name++;
        tmp_size += cb + 1;
    }
    return true;
}
```

The first loop counts the number of names by iterating through the name strings. Each name has a length byte followed by that many characters. `next = buf[3 + tmp_size] + 1` gives the total entry size (length byte + data).

The second loop reads the names. It reads `cb = buf[3 + tmp_size]` as the length. If `cb < 1`, it skips. Otherwise it allocates `cb + 1` bytes and copies `cb` bytes from `buf + 3 + tmp_size + 1`.

The check `if ((tmp_size + 4 + cb) < buf_size)` ensures we don't read past the buffer. But note: this check uses `buf_size` (the remaining buffer size passed to the function), not `record->size + 3`.

Now, the key question: what if `record->size` is larger than `buf_size - 3`? This shouldn't happen because `load_record_omf` checks:
```c
if (((OMF_record *)new)->size > buf_size - 3 || buf_size < 4) {
```

